WEBVTT

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:29.970
<v Ira Glass>So this week, lots of people looked at the election results and thought, yeah, this is the country that I thought it was. And for some people, that was a really hopeful thing, and others, kind of the opposite. And let's start with the winners. I'm joined in the studio right now by Zoe Chace.
<v Ira Glass>本周，许多人看着选举结果心想，这就是我心目中的国家。对一些人来说，这充满希望；而对另一些人来说，情况恰恰相反。让我们先从获胜者说起，现在演播室里和我在一起的是佐伊·查斯。

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:30.440
<v Zoe Chace>Hello.
<v Zoe Chace>你好。

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:37.350
<v Ira Glass>Hey there, Zoe. So election night, you and I were in Michigan together, and you got to go to the Republican victory party.
<v Ira Glass>你好，佐伊。选举之夜，你和我一起在密歇根州，你还去了共和党的胜选派对。

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:37.980
<v Zoe Chace>I did.
<v Zoe Chace>是的，我去过。

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:38.740
<v Ira Glass>Let's bring in the sound.
<v Ira Glass>让我们听听那里的声音。

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:39.860
<v Woman>Waiting for anything substantive--
<v Woman>等待任何实质性的进展——

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:40.410
<v Ira Glass>There it is.
<v Ira Glass>就在那儿。

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:52.050
<v Zoe Chace>As I walk in, the polls were closing. The results were starting to come in. I have to say, I walked in kind of prepared for the last war, like the last election.
<v Zoe Chace>我走进去的时候，投票站正在关闭。结果开始陆续出来。我必须说，我走进去的时候还带着上一场战争的心态，就像上次选举那样。

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:52.920
<v Ira Glass>Explain what you mean.
<v Ira Glass>解释一下你的意思。

00:00:52.920 --> 00:01:12.490
<v Zoe Chace>Well, I know a lot of these Republicans in Michigan. I know they've been mobilizing, for years, an army of poll watchers and poll challengers and lawyers to challenge the results of a fraudulent election. So I come in expecting super tight results, and a long fight, and tension, and anxiety, and suspicion.
<v Zoe Chace>嗯，我认识密歇根州的很多共和党人。我知道他们多年来一直在动员，组织了一支由投票观察员、投票质疑员和律师组成的军队，试图挑战所谓的“欺诈选举”结果。所以我来的时候预期会是极其胶着的局势，会有漫长的争斗、紧张、焦虑和猜疑。

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:13.760
<v Zoe Chace>[CROWD CHEERING]
<v Zoe Chace>[人群欢呼]

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:14.930
<v Ira Glass>And?
<v Ira Glass>然后呢？

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:16.270
<v Zoe Chace>That is not what I found.
<v Zoe Chace>我看到的并不是这些。

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:17.030
<v Zoe Chace>[CROWD CHEERING]
<v Zoe Chace>[人群欢呼]

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:18.610
<v Zoe Chace>What are people cheering for?
<v Zoe Chace>大家在欢呼什么？

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:21.790
<v Man>We're up in Pennsylvania. We just tied in Michigan.
<v Man>我们在宾夕法尼亚州领先。我们在密歇根州刚刚追平了。

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:22.970
<v Man>[INDISTINCT CHATTER]
<v Man>[嘈杂的谈话声]

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:23.850
<v Man>Look at that.
<v Man>看那个。

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.280
<v Man>[LAUGHTER]
<v Man>[笑声]

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:26.950
<v Man>I'm so happy.
<v Man>我太高兴了。

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:28.670
<v Man>[INDISTINCT CHATTER]
<v Man>[嘈杂的谈话声]

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:30.840
<v Man>My heart's going to pop out of my chest.
<v Man>我的心都要跳出来了。

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:31.700
<v Zoe Chace>Really?
<v Zoe Chace>真的吗？

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.320
<v Eric Castiglia>Feels like it.
<v Eric Castiglia>感觉是这样。

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:50.210
<v Zoe Chace>It's Eric Castiglia, longtime Republican activist. He runs a PAC called Brighter Michigan. He believes the last election was rife with fraud-- not this one. In fact, there was never a moment, once the count started, where anyone in this room wasn't believing the results.
<v Zoe Chace>他是埃里克·卡斯蒂利亚，一位长期的共和党活动人士。他经营着一个名为“更光明密歇根”的政治行动委员会。他认为上次选举充满了欺诈，但这次不是。事实上，当计票开始后，房间里没有任何一刻是怀疑选举结果的。

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.590
<v Ira Glass>Well, yeah, because it was all breaking their way.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，是的，因为局势完全朝着他们有利的方向发展。

00:01:52.590 --> 00:02:01.170
<v Zoe Chace>I know, but also, these are the people on the ground who ran the ground game in Michigan, and they were feeling really good about what they did.
<v Zoe Chace>我知道，但这些人也是密歇根州一线选战的组织者，他们对自己所做的工作感到非常满意。

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.630
<v Zoe Chace>All right, Eric. What's going to happen?
<v Zoe Chace>好了，埃里克。接下来会发生什么？

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:14.310
<v Eric Castiglia>I think he's going to win, baby. He's going to win. I'm so happy. I am so happy, Zoe. You have no idea.
<v Eric Castiglia>我认为他会赢的，宝贝。他会赢。我太高兴了。我真的太高兴了，佐伊。你根本无法想象。

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:17.580
<v Ira Glass>So it was obvious to them they were going to win way before it was obvious to you.
<v Ira Glass>所以在你意识到他们会赢之前，他们早就心知肚明了。

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:24.470
<v Zoe Chace>Yes, very much. Like, when I was talking to Amber Harris-- we first met last year. She's a Republican activist.
<v Zoe Chace>是的，非常清楚。比如，当我与安布尔·哈里斯交谈时——我们是去年第一次见面。她也是一名共和党活动人士。

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:27.690
<v Zoe Chace>I didn't honestly expect the night to go this way in general.
<v Zoe Chace>老实说，我根本没想到昨晚会是这个走向。

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:29.330
<v Amber Harris>Oh. I did.
<v Amber Harris>哦，我预料到了。

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:29.780
<v Zoe Chace>Really?
<v Zoe Chace>真的吗？

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:57.820
<v Amber Harris>I did, yeah. We worked really hard for this. And I'm happy. I'm very happy. We worked really, really hard. We knocked a lot of doors. We went into territories that we never would have, whether it was Detroit-- because we had nothing to lose. As a party, we have nothing to lose besides talking to people. And I think that this is a very-- this is what came when you just talk to people. [LAUGHS] Like--
<v Amber Harris>是的，预料到了。我们为此付出了巨大的努力。我很高兴。我非常高兴。我们真的工作得非常辛苦。我们挨家挨户敲门。我们去了那些从未涉足的地区，比如底特律——因为我们没什么可失去的。作为一个政党，除了与民众交谈，我们一无所有。我认为这就是当你真正与民众交谈时所产生的结果。[笑] 就像——

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:10.740
<v Zoe Chace>She's right that more people came out. The Republican vote in Michigan was unprecedented. Trump got more votes than he did last time he ran in Detroit, in the surrounding suburbs, in almost every county in the state of Michigan.
<v Zoe Chace>她说得对，确实有更多人出来了。共和党在密歇根州的投票率是空前的。特朗普在底特律、周边郊区，以及密歇根州几乎每一个县的得票数都比他上次参选时要多。

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:14.010
<v Ira Glass>OK. So then, at some point in the night, Trump wins.
<v Ira Glass>好的。那么，在那天晚上的某个时候，特朗普赢了。

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:16.560
<v Zoe Chace>Yes, much earlier than I was expecting.
<v Zoe Chace>是的，比我预期的要早得多。

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:17.790
<v Ira Glass>Let's hear some sound of that.
<v Ira Glass>让我们听听当时的场景。

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:18.230
<v Man>Yes!
<v Man>耶！

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:19.680
<v Man>[CROWD CHEERING]
<v Man>[人群欢呼]

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:21.660
<v Man>Trump! Trump! Trump! Trump!
<v Man>特朗普！特朗普！特朗普！特朗普！

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:22.490
<v Ira Glass>Thank you.
<v Ira Glass>谢谢。

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:29.100
<v Zoe Chace>And I see a guy breaking down crying at the front of the room. He's the regional director for Macomb County.
<v Zoe Chace>我看到一个男人在房间前面泪流满面。他是马科姆县的地区主管。

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:31.460
<v Zoe Chace>What was it that you think did it?
<v Zoe Chace>你认为是什么促成了这一切？

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:32.520
<v Man>What did it?
<v Man>什么促成的？

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:32.720
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah.
<v Zoe Chace>对。

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:54.540
<v Eric Castiglia>I think people realized that we're better off with President Trump, that he provided a great, great way of living for us, that he provided a way for us to provide for our families. And I think people miss that. I think people truly miss that. I think they just yearn to go back to where we were. And I think people believed in him.
<v Eric Castiglia>我认为人们意识到在特朗普总统的领导下我们会过得更好，他为我们提供了非常棒的生活方式，他让我们有能力养活家人。我觉得人们怀念那种生活。我真的觉得人们怀念那种生活。我觉得他们只是渴望回到过去的日子。而且我认为人们相信他。

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:59.350
<v Ira Glass>And that's true-- some people. And some did not.
<v Ira Glass>那确实是真的——对一些人来说。但也有一些人并不这么想。

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:04.860
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah, I believe I've heard that.
<v Zoe Chace>是的，我相信我听过这种声音。

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:29.600
<v Ira Glass>Somebody I had checked with a couple of days after the election was Danny Hodges. He's a Washington, DC, policeman who was one of the officers who defended the Capitol building back on January 6. He was beaten. Somebody tried to gouge out his eye. He was trapped by the crowd at one point, and a man took his nightstick and hit his head. In the wake of that, he's testified in Congress and at the trials of a number of people who attacked the Capitol, including one last week.
<v Ira Glass>选举后几天，我联系过丹尼·霍奇斯。他是一位华盛顿特区的警察，是1月6日保卫国会大厦的警官之一。他当时遭到了殴打。有人试图抠出他的眼睛。他一度被人群困住，一名男子抢走了他的警棍并击打他的头部。在那之后，他在国会作证，并在针对袭击国会的多名参与者的审判中出庭，包括上周的一场审判。

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:40.740
<v Ira Glass>And when I talked to him, he said something that I heard from a bunch of Democrats. It's like, it wasn't just disappointment he was feeling, but a whole feeling that I can only describe as "whoa."
<v Ira Glass>当我与他交谈时，他说出了一些我从许多民主党人那里听到的想法。那不仅是失望，而是一种我只能用“哇”来形容的复杂情绪。

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:56.790
<v Danny Hodges>Like many people, I'm sad and confused. I'm still wrapping my head around how so many people could vote for Trump after everything we know about him, everything he's done. I don't understand it. I don't get it.
<v Danny Hodges>和许多人一样，我感到悲伤和困惑。我仍然无法理解，在了解了他的一切作为之后，为什么还有这么多人投票给特朗普。我不理解。我不明白。

00:04:56.790 --> 00:05:08.890
<v Ira Glass>Since January 6, you've spoken so much about what happened to you at the Capitol-- over four years. When you started on that path, what did you hope it would accomplish?
<v Ira Glass>自1月6日以来，你多次谈到你在国会大厦经历的事情——已经过去四年了。当你开始这条路时，你希望达成什么目标？

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:49.590
<v Danny Hodges>[LAUGHS] The opposite of this. I hoped that I would communicate to the people that what happened was real, that Trump sent an armed mob to stop the peaceful transfer of power. I wanted people to understand this, and in the hope that they would respect the rule of law and that they would have respect for the Constitution and choose their leaders with that in mind in the future. But I guess that wasn't at the top of everyone's priorities.
<v Danny Hodges>[笑] 恰恰相反的结果。我希望向人们传达那天发生的一切是真实的，特朗普派遣了武装暴徒来阻止权力的和平移交。我希望人们能理解这一点，并希望他们尊重法治，尊重宪法，在未来以此为前提选择领导人。但我想这并不是每个人的首要任务。

00:05:49.590 --> 00:06:00.630
<v Ira Glass>And what's it like to look at the possibility that President Trump might do what he said he would do and let all these people free from January 6?
<v Ira Glass>如果特朗普总统真的履行诺言，赦免所有1月6日事件的参与者，你对此有何看法？

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:16.290
<v Danny Hodges>It's hard to process. I don't know. I don't know what to say about that except that I have no influence over that and I've just got to roll with it, however it happens.
<v Danny Hodges>这很难消化。我不知道。除了说我对此毫无影响力，必须顺其自然之外，我不知道该说什么。

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:40.290
<v Ira Glass>Today, after the billions of dollars spent and the countless hours of literally hundreds of thousands around the country who knocked themselves out for this election canvassing, donating, poll watching, doing everything they knew how to do, now, here we are, still a divided country, staring at each other across an abyss, but with a clear winner.
<v Ira Glass>今天，在投入了数十亿美元，全国数十万人为这场选举不遗余力地进行拉票、捐款、观察投票、做尽了他们能做的一切之后，我们依然是一个分裂的国家，隔着深渊彼此凝视，但有了一个明确的赢家。

00:06:40.290 --> 00:07:11.700
<v Ira Glass>We thought what we could do here on our show is spend some time with a few of the people who felt very strongly about the outcome of this race, who have a special, personal investment in it, looking ahead with them to what's next. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>我们觉得可以在节目里花点时间采访一些对这场竞选结果深感关切、有着特殊个人投入的人，展望他们的未来。来自芝加哥WBEZ广播电台，这里是《美国生活》，我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:42.930
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life, Act One-- our act names today, by the way, are quotes from the president-elect-- "The Largest Deportation Operation in American History." So mass deportations are coming. Donald Trump promised it. People waved mass deportation signs at his rallies. It was the first item on the Republican Party's platform this year. And at the Republican National Convention, Tom Homan, the guy who ran ICE under Trump, the Immigration and Customs Enforcement, had a message for undocumented immigrants-- you'd better start packing now.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》，第一幕——顺便说一下，我们今天的节目名称取自当选总统的语录——“美国历史上最大的驱逐行动”。所以，大规模驱逐行动即将到来。唐纳德·特朗普承诺过。在他的竞选集会上，人们挥舞着大规模驱逐的标语。这是今年共和党纲领的第一项内容。在共和党全国代表大会上，特朗普任内的ICE（移民及海关执法局）局长汤姆·霍曼向非法移民发出了一条信息——你们最好现在就开始打包。

00:07:42.930 --> 00:08:06.960
<v Ira Glass>President Trump has claimed he's going to be able to deport between 15 and 20 million people. His Vice President, JD Vance, said the number is lower, that they could do one million people per year. But Donald Trump and his team have never spelled out exactly how they would do this. Our producer, Nadia Reiman, wondered about that, and looked into what mass deportation could actually look like if and when it comes to pass.
<v Ira Glass>特朗普总统声称他将能够驱逐1500万到2000万人。他的副手万斯说数字会少一些，他们一年可以驱逐100万人。但唐纳德·特朗普及其团队从未明确说明他们将如何实施这一计划。我们的制作人纳迪亚·雷曼对此感到好奇，并深入调查了如果大规模驱逐行动真正发生时会是什么样子。

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:34.620
<v Nadia Reiman>I was kind of unfazed at first when I heard about Trump's mass deportation campaign promise. I've been covering immigration for years. There are so many undocumented people here that we can't even keep count. The closest estimate is 13.3 million. So it's a big job to round up all of these people and to send them back to countries that may not take them. It's expensive. It's time-consuming. It's a crazy task.
<v Nadia Reiman>刚听到特朗普的大规模驱逐竞选承诺时，我最初并没有太在意。我报道移民问题多年了。这里的非法移民人数多到我们甚至无法统计。最接近的估计是1330万。所以，把这些人全部找出来并遣返回那些可能根本不接收他们的国家，是一项巨大的工程。这昂贵、耗时，简直是疯狂的举动。

00:08:34.620 --> 00:09:00.330
<v Nadia Reiman>But then I was talking to a source inside the Department of Homeland Security. And I said something like, I mean, I know there's no way Trump can do this mass deportation thing. And they stopped me. They were like, oh, sure, it can happen. They said it so casually, like it was a given. So I wanted to talk to someone who could break down how. Like, exactly how could you do something so massive?
<v Nadia Reiman>但后来我与国土安全部的一位消息人士交谈。我说，我的意思是，我知道特朗普根本不可能实施这种大规模驱逐。他们打断了我。他们说，噢，当然，这完全可能发生。他们说得那么随意，就像这是一件理所当然的事。所以我想要找一个能拆解具体实施方法的人。比如，究竟该如何完成如此庞大的任务？

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:22.950
<v Nadia Reiman>I tried talking to Tom Homan. He's rumored to be the next ICE director under Trump but has denied there's a written plan for mass deportations. And he ghosted me. Jason Houser, however, was eager to talk. He was the chief of staff for ICE under Biden for a couple years, has been working for DHS on and off since 9/11, mostly in enforcement.
<v Nadia Reiman>我尝试联系汤姆·霍曼。有传言称他是特朗普政府下任ICE局长的候选人，但他否认有关于大规模驱逐的书面计划。他没理我。然而，杰森·豪泽非常乐意交谈。他是拜登政府期间ICE的幕僚长，自9/11事件以来，他一直在国土安全部断断续续地工作，主要负责执法。

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:42.600
<v Nadia Reiman>He's passionate about all of this, in his wonky, government guy way. I talked to him for 3.5 hours, asked him basically to play pretend, game some stuff out with me. What would happen in the first 100 days? If he was in charge of ICE, what could he do to carry out this mass deportation mandate?
<v Nadia Reiman>他以那种官僚主义的、精明的风格，对这一切充满热情。我与他交谈了3.5个小时，基本上请求他和我进行模拟推演。如果头100天会发生什么？如果他是ICE的负责人，他能做些什么来执行这项大规模驱逐指令？

00:09:42.600 --> 00:10:11.400
<v Nadia Reiman>Jason thinks that, first of all, the new Trump administration will immediately start to prep for this-- like, the day after the election, this week. They would start talking to law enforcement in different cities and getting them to agree to cooperate, hit the ground running in January. ICE would talk to home countries to get them to agree to take people back. And after those two things align, Jason says, ICE could decide to deport someone and they'd be out of the country within 24 hours.
<v Nadia Reiman>杰森认为，首先，特朗普新政府会立即为此做准备——比如选举后的第二天，即本周。他们会开始与不同城市的执法部门沟通，争取合作，并在1月份开始实施。ICE会与来源国沟通，要求他们同意接收遣返人员。当这两件事协调一致后，杰森说，ICE可以决定驱逐某人，并在24小时内将其送出境。

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:32.190
<v Jason Houser>I think the first 90 days is going to be hell. You're going to see the buses. You're going to see the migrants in your home-- not just blue cities, red cities-- Miami, Houston, Charlotte-- like, red states-- Kansas City, St. Louis.
<v Jason Houser>我认为前90天将是地狱。你会看到大巴车。你会看到移民就在你家附近——不仅是在民主党控制的蓝市，还有红色城市——迈阿密、休斯顿、夏洛特——甚至是红州——堪萨斯城、圣路易斯。

00:10:32.190 --> 00:10:50.630
<v Jason Houser>You're going to see kids not in your schools. You're going to know where they're at because they're waiting in a detention cell and they have cell phones. You're going to see it in social media. You're going to see businesses not be able to open up because their workers didn't show up. You're going to see businesses being raided. And it's going to become more intimate.
<v Jason Houser>你会发现孩子们不再出现在学校里。你会知道他们在哪，因为他们正被关在拘留所里，手里拿着手机。你会在社交媒体上看到这一切。你会看到企业因为员工没来上班而无法开业。你会看到企业被突袭。这一切将变得更加切身。

00:10:50.630 --> 00:11:06.400
<v Jason Houser>This isn't going to be about separating a family at the border, that somebody doesn't know that family member. You're talking about separations and movements in your communities where you're going to know the guy-- Bill, Juan, Luis. You're going to know the individuals.
<v Jason Houser>这不再只是边境上的家庭分离，那是你不认识的某个家庭。这说的是发生在你社区里的分离和人员迁徙，你会认识那个家伙——比尔、胡安、路易斯。你会认识这些个体。

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:25.870
<v Nadia Reiman>One of the things ICE would have to work around is which nationalities to deport first-- you know, if you wanted to make the most impact. A lot of countries don't take their own people back. Venezuela, for example, hardly accepts any immigrants back. Brazil only accepts two to four flights of immigrants a month.
<v Nadia Reiman>ICE需要克服的问题之一是优先驱逐哪些国籍的人——你知道，如果你想产生最大的影响的话。许多国家不接收自己的国民。例如，委内瑞拉几乎不接收任何被驱逐的移民。巴西每月只接受两到四架移民遣返航班。

00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:29.630
<v Nadia Reiman>So who are the first people who would be deported, like, in the first hundred days?
<v Nadia Reiman>那么，谁是前100天里最先被驱逐的人？

00:11:29.630 --> 00:11:33.250
<v Jason Houser>Haitians and Guatemalans.
<v Jason Houser>海地人和危地马拉人。

00:11:33.250 --> 00:11:35.710
<v Nadia Reiman>Haitians and Guatemalans?
<v Nadia Reiman>海地人和危地马拉人？

00:11:35.710 --> 00:11:53.260
<v Jason Houser>These countries take back the most flights. So I have an ability to remove-- at volume-- individuals. A lot of them have entered through pathways that we've developed, where we've gained biometric, we've gained vetting, and we've--
<v Jason Houser>这些国家接收的航班最多。所以我能够大规模地遣返个人。其中很多人是通过我们开发的途径进来的，我们掌握了生物识别信息，进行了审查，而且我们——

00:11:53.260 --> 00:11:54.620
<v Nadia Reiman>Meaning we know where they are.
<v Nadia Reiman>意味着我们知道他们在哪里。

00:11:54.620 --> 00:12:20.120
<v Jason Houser>Yeah, we know where they are. We know where they are. They're working, non-criminal. We go out, we find them. We're going to find nationalities that are easily removable. And we're probably going to do it at volume with single adults first, because removing families is more complex. You've got to hold them, detain them, put them in hotels. It's very staff-intensive. So I'm going to target down on single adults and nationalities where I know the country will take them back very, very quickly.
<v Jason Houser>是的，我们知道他们在哪里。我们知道他们在哪里。他们正在工作，是非刑事犯罪人员。我们出去找到他们。我们可能会优先寻找容易遣返国籍的人。我们可能会先针对单身成年人进行大规模行动，因为遣返家庭更复杂。你必须关押他们、拘留他们，把他们安置在旅馆里。这非常耗费人力。所以我将把重点放在单身成年人身上，以及那些我确定该国会迅速接收他们的国籍人群。

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:23.350
<v Nadia Reiman>OK. So Haitians and Guatemalans and then--
<v Nadia Reiman>好的。所以是海地人和危地马拉人，然后——

00:12:23.350 --> 00:12:24.860
<v Jason Houser>There's other populations too, but--
<v Jason Houser>还有其他群体，但——

00:12:24.860 --> 00:12:25.990
<v Nadia Reiman>Who's next after those two?
<v Nadia Reiman>在这两个群体之后是谁？

00:12:25.990 --> 00:12:43.130
<v Jason Houser>Hondurans, right? We talk about Guatemalans, Hondurans, Nicaraguans, the countries where you see daily-- just in the news and the reporting by DHS and Homeland Security-- where are the most removal flights occurring? Those are the countries that accept those flights.
<v Jason Houser>洪都拉斯人，对吧？我们谈论危地马拉人、洪都拉斯人、尼加拉瓜人，这些国家每天都在新闻和国土安全部的报告中被提及——哪里发生的遣返航班最多？那些就是接收航班的国家。

00:12:43.130 --> 00:13:08.710
<v Jason Houser>But if I'm in this scenario where I'm the head of ICE for Trump, all the rules of engagement and policies are out the window. Why not load up a few planeloads of Cuban nationals and send them to the Bahamas and just send them to a third party? Why not just-- I could go find a country that says they'll accept three or four planeloads of Cuban nationals, and I'll send them to a third-party country.
<v Jason Houser>但如果我身处这个情境中，我是特朗普治下的ICE负责人，所有的交战规则和政策都被抛到了窗外。为什么不装满几飞机古巴人，把他们送到巴哈马，送给第三方？为什么不——我可以找一个愿意接收三四飞机古巴人的国家，然后把他们送到第三方国家。

00:13:08.710 --> 00:13:28.240
<v Nadia Reiman>Jason says ICE can focus on getting people in eight major cities with airport hubs-- Philadelphia, DC, Chicago, Houston, Miami, Denver, New York, and LA. Those have access to quick ICE flights to people's home countries. Jason thinks ICE can get people out before they can get to a judge or a lawyer.
<v Nadia Reiman>杰森说ICE可以专注于拥有机场枢纽的八个主要城市——费城、华盛顿特区、芝加哥、休斯顿、迈阿密、丹佛、纽约和洛杉矶。这些地方可以快速安排ICE航班前往遣返对象的祖国。杰森认为ICE可以在这些人接触到法官或律师之前就把他们送走。

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:42.490
<v Nadia Reiman>And one way to catch a lot of people quickly, especially the ones who snuck in and ICE has no info on, is to bring back worksite enforcement, a.k.a., large-scale raids. The Biden administration stopped doing those.
<v Nadia Reiman>而快速抓捕大量人群——尤其是那些潜入且ICE没有任何记录的人——的一个方法是恢复工作场所执法，即大规模突袭。拜登政府已经停止了这样做。

00:13:42.490 --> 00:13:46.670
<v Nadia Reiman>Do you think there would be raids, then, in the first 100 days?
<v Nadia Reiman>那你认为前100天内会发生突袭吗？

00:13:46.670 --> 00:13:49.210
<v Jason Houser>I think there would be raids within the first three weeks.
<v Jason Houser>我认为在最初的三周内就会有突袭。

00:13:49.210 --> 00:13:50.080
<v Nadia Reiman>Really?
<v Nadia Reiman>真的吗？

00:13:50.080 --> 00:13:56.470
<v Jason Houser>Yeah. Those are not hard to turn on. Like, to operationalize those, those aren't hard.
<v Jason Houser>是的。启动这些并不难。要将其投入运作，并不难。

00:13:56.470 --> 00:14:00.570
<v Nadia Reiman>Where? Where would they do them?
<v Nadia Reiman>在哪里？他们会在哪里进行？

00:14:00.570 --> 00:14:27.260
<v Jason Houser>You would go back to where there's big ICE and Customs and Border Protection resources to do enforcement. And you would do them in communities that would show the most cruelty. So there's nothing that would stop a Trump administration from going into the workplace, going into our hospitality sector, going into restaurants or businesses, and arresting individuals at scale.
<v Jason Houser>你会回到那些拥有大型ICE和海关与边境保护局资源来进行执法的地方。而且你会在那些最能体现残酷性的社区里进行。因此，没有任何东西能阻止特朗普政府进入工作场所、进入我们的服务业、进入餐馆或企业，并进行大规模的个人逮捕。

00:14:27.260 --> 00:14:30.710
<v Nadia Reiman>Can you walk through what that would look like? What do you think that would look like?
<v Nadia Reiman>你能演示一下那是什么样子的吗？你认为会是什么场景？

00:14:30.710 --> 00:14:54.710
<v Jason Houser>Well, I think it would be very easy to focus on industries that have large numbers and high numbers of migrants working within them. What would stop them from going into a meat processing plant in Virginia? Say there's a couple hundred migrants. There's 80 on shift that day. You go in, you know there's one individual there that has a final order of removal, maybe has a nonviolent criminal background.
<v Jason Houser>嗯，我认为专注于雇佣了大量移民的行业是非常容易的。有什么能阻止他们进入弗吉尼亚州的一家肉类加工厂呢？假设有几百名移民，那天正好有80人在上班。你进去，你知道那里有一个有最终驱逐令的人，可能还有非暴力的犯罪背景。

00:14:54.710 --> 00:15:17.560
<v Jason Houser>You go in, you do the raid, you line all the workers up, and you start checking status of each and every one of them, right? Or maybe you just arrest them all, bring them into detention, and then do the checks to see who is removable. There's nothing that could stop ICE, at that point, from just bringing people into custody, detaining them, and then figuring out who is removable at that time.
<v Jason Houser>你进去，进行突袭，让所有工人排好队，开始核查每一个人的身份，对吧？或者你可能直接把他们全部逮捕，带进拘留所，然后再做核查看看谁是可以被驱逐的。在那一点上，没有什么能阻止ICE直接将人拘留、关押，然后再弄清楚谁是可以驱逐的。

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:51.070
<v Nadia Reiman>Tom Homan has not denied this, by the way. He's said publicly something like this would be necessary. Homan also said he would do national security threats first-- but then raids, sure. Jason says the raids under a 2.0 Trump administration could be more militarized, with SWAT-style teams. That's not how they've been done in the past. He also told me he thinks nothing would stop ICE from going into hospitals or schools or churches. Normally, ICE doesn't do that. But this is just a policy, not a law.
<v Nadia Reiman>顺便说一句，汤姆·霍曼并没有否认这一点。他在公开场合说过，这样做是必要的。霍曼还说，他会先针对国家安全威胁——但随后是突袭，当然。杰森说，特朗普2.0政府下的突袭可能会更加军事化，配有特警式团队。过去的情况并非如此。他还告诉我，他认为没有什么能阻止ICE进入医院、学校或教堂。通常情况下，ICE不会那样做。但这只是政策，不是法律。

00:15:51.070 --> 00:16:03.460
<v Nadia Reiman>So after doing these raids and trying to get people out quickly, ICE would be left with lots of immigrants that they have to hold until they can get them deported. I wanted to know, where would you hold all these people?
<v Nadia Reiman>所以在进行了这些突袭并试图快速遣送人员之后，ICE将会剩下许多必须关押直到能够驱逐的移民。我想知道，你会把这些人关在哪里？

00:16:03.460 --> 00:16:07.150
<v Nadia Reiman>Would you build tent camps to hold people?
<v Nadia Reiman>你会建造帐篷营地来关押人吗？

00:16:07.150 --> 00:16:27.620
<v Jason Houser>If I'm not looking out for the care of the law enforcement officers that are overseeing it or the migrants, yes, you could. When we did all the evacs and ICE had a very big support in the evacuation out of Afghanistan, we brought on soft-sided facilities at Fort Dix, New Jersey, and other places around the country. But again--
<v Jason Houser>如果我不考虑执法人员或移民的照顾问题，是的，可以。当我们进行所有撤离行动时，ICE在阿富汗撤离行动中得到了非常大的支持，我们在新泽西州迪克斯堡和全国其他地方启用了软质设施。但再次强调——

00:16:27.620 --> 00:16:30.860
<v Nadia Reiman>Soft-sided facilities? Is that euphemism for tents?
<v Nadia Reiman>软质设施？这是帐篷的委婉说法吗？

00:16:30.860 --> 00:16:43.760
<v Jason Houser>Well, yes. They're big, sort of like fair-- like, carnival tents. They're very thick plastic, and they're soft. Yeah. But what I'm saying is, let's say we put a thousand--
<v Jason Houser>嗯，是的。它们很大，有点像集市——像嘉年华帐篷。它们是很厚的塑料，是软的。是的。但我说的是，假设我们要安置一千人——

00:16:43.760 --> 00:16:45.610
<v Nadia Reiman>You're like, they're weatherproof. They're weatherproof. But they're tents.
<v Nadia Reiman>你就像在说，它们是防风雨的。它们是防风雨的。但它们就是帐篷。

00:16:45.610 --> 00:17:04.790
<v Jason Houser>Well, they're not just, like, Coleman tents that you would get to go camping. But what I'm saying is-- but the problem here is, those Afghanis were coming to safety. Now, you're saying, I'm going to bring on a soft-side tent to hold people so I can remove them in, like, 90 days.
<v Jason Houser>嗯，它们不像那种你去露营时买的科尔曼帐篷。但我说的是——问题在于，那些阿富汗人是来寻求安全的。现在你却说，我要启用软质帐篷来关押人，这样我可以在90天内把他们移走。

00:17:04.790 --> 00:17:16.690
<v Jason Houser>The idea that they're just going to, like-- OK, I'm going to live in this deplorable conditions and not cause unrest-- that's where it gets very dangerous.
<v Jason Houser>他们会安分守己地生活在这种凄惨的环境中，不引起动乱——那种想法才最危险。

00:17:16.690 --> 00:17:35.170
<v Nadia Reiman>Dangerous because Jason thinks there would be fights, riots. People would be hurt, possibly even die, he thinks. And just so I'm clear here, yes, he means immigrants, but also officers. Jason's very concerned about the safety of the ICE officers.
<v Nadia Reiman>危险是因为杰森认为会发生斗殴、暴乱。他认为人们会受伤，甚至可能死亡。我想澄清一下，是的，他说的是移民，但还有警员。杰森非常关心ICE警员的安全。

00:17:35.170 --> 00:17:40.120
<v Nadia Reiman>Where else can you hold people you want to deport? Can you house migrants in jails?
<v Nadia Reiman>你还可以把想要驱逐的人关在什么地方？你可以把移民安置在监狱里吗？

00:17:40.120 --> 00:17:41.630
<v Jason Houser>Yes.
<v Jason Houser>可以。

00:17:41.630 --> 00:17:46.220
<v Nadia Reiman>Could you lower jailing standards to put more people in a cell?
<v Nadia Reiman>你可以降低监狱标准，把更多人塞进一个牢房吗？

00:17:46.220 --> 00:17:49.460
<v Jason Houser>Yes. Well, yes, absolutely.
<v Jason Houser>可以。嗯，是的，绝对可以。

00:17:49.460 --> 00:17:49.750
<v Nadia Reiman>Right.
<v Nadia Reiman>对。

00:17:49.750 --> 00:17:50.040
<v Jason Houser>But yes.
<v Jason Houser>但是的。

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:54.540
<v Nadia Reiman>Like, I'm saying, could you-- our standard before was three people per cell, and now, we're saying it's six.
<v Nadia Reiman>我是说，你可以——我们之前的标准是每个牢房三个人，现在我们说它是六个。

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:57.770
<v Jason Houser>That is policy. It is not law. So yes, of course you could.
<v Jason Houser>那是政策。不是法律。所以是的，当然可以。

00:17:57.770 --> 00:18:08.060
<v Nadia Reiman>OK. Could you turn places quickly into detention centers or camps? Like, can you change the standards to make it faster?
<v Nadia Reiman>好的。你可以快速地把地方变成拘留中心或营地吗？比如，你可以改变标准来加快速度吗？

00:18:08.060 --> 00:18:20.390
<v Jason Houser>Yes. I could have-- between FEMA and ICE and DHS, we could turn on 25 old warehouses, old department stores in a week.
<v Jason Houser>可以。我可以在一周内动用联邦紧急事务管理局、ICE和国土安全部，启用25个旧仓库、旧百货商店。

00:18:20.390 --> 00:18:23.030
<v Nadia Reiman>Wow, in a week.
<v Nadia Reiman>哇，一周之内。

00:18:23.030 --> 00:18:23.290
<v Jason Houser>Well--
<v Jason Houser>嗯——

00:18:23.290 --> 00:18:25.840
<v Nadia Reiman>So definitely doable in the first 100 days.
<v Nadia Reiman>所以在前100天内绝对可以做到。

00:18:25.840 --> 00:18:44.810
<v Jason Houser>--it would just be a cement building with some mats. And then I make sure we have porta-potties, some food. I have a couple docs there in case somebody gets really sick. And then I bring in a couple hundred security guards. I could do that in a week.
<v Jason Houser>——它只是一个水泥建筑，铺上一些垫子。然后我确保有移动厕所，一些食物。放几个医生在场，以防有人病得很重。然后再调来几百名保安。我一周内就能搞定。

00:18:44.810 --> 00:18:55.190
<v Nadia Reiman>At the end of the 100 days, how many people do you think will be gone?
<v Nadia Reiman>在100天结束时，你认为会有多少人离开？

00:18:55.190 --> 00:19:30.830
<v Jason Houser>Let's just say this. Let's say all rules are out the-- and I can remove people that aren't removable. Like, I'm going to send them to third-party countries. ICE has 48,000 people in its custody now. ICE has 14 ICE planes that are hardened planes. They hold 135-- 135 souls. I need more of those. But while I'm sending those 48,000, I'm probably going to go out and bring another 50,000 to 100,000 into custody. So if you're talking 30 to 60 days, you could remove 150,000 to 200,000 people.
<v Jason Houser>我们这样说吧。假设所有的规则都——我可以移走那些不可驱逐的人。比如，我把他们送到第三方国家。ICE目前有48,000人在其监管下。ICE有14架强化飞机。它们可容纳135人——135个灵魂。我需要更多这样的飞机。但在我遣送那48,000人的同时，我可能会出去再带5万到10万人进入拘留所。所以如果你谈论的是30到60天，你可以驱逐15万到20万人。

00:19:30.830 --> 00:19:33.290
<v Nadia Reiman>So 200,000 people in the first 60 days?
<v Nadia Reiman>所以在前60天内有20万人？

00:19:33.290 --> 00:19:33.680
<v Jason Houser>Yeah.
<v Jason Houser>是的。

00:19:33.680 --> 00:19:40.940
<v Nadia Reiman>So in the first 100, that puts you at what, how many?
<v Nadia Reiman>所以在前100天内，那加起来是多少人？

00:19:40.940 --> 00:19:47.090
<v Jason Houser>If all rules are gone and I can remove them anywhere, you could do a million.
<v Jason Houser>如果所有的规则都没了，我可以把他们移到任何地方，你可以驱逐一百万。

00:19:47.090 --> 00:20:10.290
<v Nadia Reiman>A million people. Of course, Jason's predicting here, assuming there will be no major roadblocks. But the Brennan Center did this thing where they stress tested with experts and government people whether mass deportations could be done, gamed this all out. In their simulations, funding was a big obstacle right away, so their deportation numbers weren't as large as Jason's.
<v Nadia Reiman>一百万人。当然，杰森在这里的预测是假设没有任何重大障碍。但是布伦南中心做过一项研究，他们与专家和政府人员进行了压力测试，看看大规模驱逐是否可行。在他们的模拟中，资金是一个很大的即时障碍，所以他们的驱逐数字没有杰森的那么大。

00:20:10.290 --> 00:20:29.030
<v Nadia Reiman>But that was also assuming that the House wouldn't go Republican, which is looking like it will be as I record this. That would make Jason's math of a million people more possible. And when a million people disappear from the country, it's more than just bodies gone. There's ripple effects.
<v Nadia Reiman>但那也是假设众议院不会被共和党控制，而正如我录音时显示的那样，这看起来将会发生。这会使杰森的一百万人计算变得更可能。当一百万人从国家中消失时，这不仅仅是肉体消失了。这会产生连锁反应。

00:20:29.030 --> 00:20:44.130
<v Jason Houser>One, we'll see massive inflation continue in this country, because we just pulled a million people out of our workforce-- GDP, businesses, small business especially. And then we'll see thousands of people losing their jobs and small businesses closing, et cetera.
<v Jason Houser>第一，我们将看到这个国家的通货膨胀大幅持续，因为我们刚刚从我们的劳动力——GDP、企业，尤其是小企业——中抽走了一百万人。然后我们将看到成千上万的人失去工作，小企业倒闭，等等。

00:20:44.130 --> 00:21:17.850
<v Jason Houser>Two, law enforcement activity-- federal specifically, and in cities and states where state and local law enforcement is supporting this mass deportation program-- will halt. Halt. Going out and arresting the rapist and murderer in your county will stop while your sheriff is over playing grabass with Homan and these individuals and trying to do some big mass deportation scheme and throwing Grandma back to Cuba. So law enforcement will be chilled.
<v Jason Houser>第二，执法活动——联邦层面，以及州和地方执法部门正在支持这一大规模驱逐计划的城市和州——将会陷入停滞。停滞。当你的治安官正忙着和霍曼以及这些人鬼混，试图搞什么大规模驱逐计划，并把老奶奶扔回古巴时，出去抓捕你县里的强奸犯和杀人犯的工作将会停止。所以执法活动会变得迟缓。

00:21:17.850 --> 00:21:42.950
<v Jason Houser>Three, migrants will go deeper into the shadows. They will do the steps they need to stay in our country, because it's so much better than going back and risking death in another that they will hide even more into the shadows.
<v Jason Houser>第三，移民将更深入地潜入阴影中。他们会采取他们需要采取的步骤留在我们国家，因为这比回去并冒着在另一个国家死亡的风险要好得多，所以他们会藏得更深。

00:21:42.950 --> 00:22:12.500
<v Nadia Reiman>And so those that are left behind, it's like they become ghosts. They might stop showing up to work. Maybe they'll move. You'll notice them missing-- at your local grocery store, your bar, your daycare. And at the same time, your screens will be broadcasting the deportations on TV, on social media, shouting the images of a million who are actually gone. Jason thinks this will be strategic.
<v Nadia Reiman>于是那些被留下的人，就像成了鬼魂。他们可能会不再出现工作。也许他们会搬走。你会注意到他们失踪了——在你当地的杂货店、酒吧、托儿所。同时，你的屏幕将会在电视上、社交媒体上广播驱逐过程，大肆宣扬那一百万已经真正离开的人的图像。杰森认为这会是策略性的。

00:22:12.500 --> 00:22:41.750
<v Jason Houser>I think there's going to be lined-up planes, engines-running imagery of mass removals. I think they're going to grab people and intentionally break our policy, like, break the law and throw some people back to their home country. And then when the courts push back, they can be like, see, the courts made us stop. Because then they can go, hey, look at these bureaucratic pencil necks getting in our way. This is the deep state. This is the deep state.
<v Jason Houser>我认为会有排队的飞机、引擎运转的图像，显示大规模驱逐。我认为他们会抓人并故意打破我们的政策，比如违法把一些人扔回他们的祖国。然后当法院进行反击时，他们可以像说，你看，是法院让我们停下的。因为他们可以去说，嘿，看看这些官僚主义的笨蛋在阻碍我们。这就是深层政府。这就是深层政府。

00:22:41.750 --> 00:22:48.320
<v Nadia Reiman>I guess my thing is, to what end, though?
<v Nadia Reiman>我大概想问的是，为了什么目的呢？

00:22:48.320 --> 00:22:50.360
<v Jason Houser>Winning the midterms.
<v Jason Houser>赢得中期选举。

00:22:50.360 --> 00:22:55.760
<v Nadia Reiman>You think it's all political? You don't think there's any ideological thing that he wants out of it?
<v Nadia Reiman>你认为这一切都是政治？你不认为他有任何想从中得到的意识形态吗？

00:22:55.760 --> 00:23:29.060
<v Jason Houser>Sure, I think he's ill-equipped. But there's 8 million just on the immigration docket now. How many people have status here and are first-generation migrants, or from migrant families? I mean, we're talking-- this would affect, like, 50 million to 100 million people, these sort of actions. The idea that there isn't political consequences to that, even for Donald Trump-- I think the pendulum would swing back.
<v Jason Houser>当然，我认为他能力不足。但单是移民案件档案里就有800万人。有多少人在这里拥有合法身份，或者是第一代移民，还是来自移民家庭？我的意思是，我们说的是——这种行动将影响约5000万到1亿人。认为这对唐纳德·特朗普也没有政治后果的想法——我认为钟摆会摆回来。

00:23:29.060 --> 00:23:54.050
<v Nadia Reiman>I'm not so sure. I think it's way harder for the pendulum to swing back. If you look at the Democrats' immigration platform, what you see is the border bill that Biden tried to pass. That bill is the most restrictive immigration bill we've seen in years. It's a child born out of the Trump administration but also parented by the Democrats, making a harsh agenda seem way more middle of the road.
<v Nadia Reiman>我不那么确定。我认为钟摆要摆回来要难得多。如果你看看民主党的移民纲领，你会看到拜登试图通过的边境法案。该法案是多年来我们见过的最严格的移民法案。它是特朗普政府的产物，但也是由民主党抚养长大的，使得强硬议程看起来更像中庸之道。

00:23:54.050 --> 00:24:21.200
<v Nadia Reiman>Think piece after think piece uses this failed bill as an example of how far to the right we've moved on immigration, how much enforcement and severity have taken over, how much the idea of a nation of immigrants is dusty and Pollyanna-ish. This fall, a Brookings poll said that about a third of Americans agree with Trump's quote, that undocumented immigrants are poisoning the blood of the country-- "poisoning the blood."
<v Nadia Reiman>一篇又一篇评论文章用这个失败的法案作为例子，说明我们在移民问题上向右移动了多少，执法和严厉程度占据了多少主导地位，说明移民国家的理念是多么陈旧和幼稚。今年秋天，布鲁金斯学会的一项民意调查显示，约三分之一的美国人同意特朗普的引语，即非法移民正在毒害这个国家的血液——“毒害血液”。

00:24:21.200 --> 00:24:47.920
<v Nadia Reiman>This phrase chills me. It's not about legality or order. It's visceral. It's guttural repulsion. It is a violent feeling about a massive group of people. And when the President is the one that leads it, it's not just a feeling anymore. It becomes an action.
<v Nadia Reiman>这句话让我不寒而栗。这与合法性或秩序无关。它是发自肺腑的。它是喉咙深处的排斥感。这是对一大群人的暴力感。当总统是那个引领它的人时，它就不再仅仅是一种感觉了。它变成了一种行动。

00:24:47.920 --> 00:24:51.190
<v Ira Glass>Nadia Reiman. She's an editor on our show.
<v Ira Glass>纳迪亚·雷曼。她是我们节目的编辑。

00:24:51.190 --> 00:25:16.340
<v Ira Glass>Two days after the election, two of our producers, Emmanuel Dzotsi and Lilly Sullivan, went to this church near her office in New York City where, on certain days, there are migrants and asylum seekers getting free legal advice and meals. And they met this guy, Roberto, who told him that he arrived in the US five months ago from Ecuador. And he's been having trouble sleeping ever since Election Day. Lilly now joins me in the studio. Hey, Lilly.
<v Ira Glass>选举两天后，我们的两位制作人伊曼纽尔·佐齐和莉莉·沙利文去了纽约市她办公室附近的一座教堂，在那里，在某些日子，有移民和寻求庇护者获得免费法律咨询和餐食。他们遇见了那个叫罗伯托的家伙，他告诉他们他五个月前从厄瓜多尔来到美国。自选举日以来，他一直难以入睡。莉莉现在加入了我们的演播室。嘿，莉莉。

00:25:16.340 --> 00:25:17.060
<v Lilly Sullivan>Hey, Ira.
<v Lilly Sullivan>嘿，艾拉。

00:25:17.060 --> 00:25:18.350
<v Ira Glass>So you spoke with him.
<v Ira Glass>所以你和他说过了。

00:25:18.350 --> 00:25:25.880
<v Lilly Sullivan>Yeah. He's been staying in a shelter nearby. And he says he can already picture it, what the ICE raids there would be like.
<v Lilly Sullivan>是的。他一直住在附近的一个收容所里。他说他已经能想象出那里的ICE突袭会是什么样子了。

00:25:25.880 --> 00:25:30.190
<v Roberto>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Roberto>[西班牙语交谈]

00:25:30.190 --> 00:25:38.620
<v Lilly Sullivan>He says, let me describe it like a movie. He pictures a group of 100, 200 men arriving, who'd lock up the building and go floor to floor.
<v Lilly Sullivan>他说，让我把它描述得像一部电影。他想象着一群100、200名男人到达，他们会封锁大楼并逐层搜查。

00:25:38.620 --> 00:25:40.660
<v Roberto>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Roberto>[西班牙语交谈]

00:25:40.660 --> 00:25:42.180
<v Lilly Sullivan>No one would be able to get out.
<v Lilly Sullivan>没有人能够出去。

00:25:42.180 --> 00:25:44.610
<v Roberto>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Roberto>[西班牙语交谈]

00:25:44.610 --> 00:25:55.480
<v Lilly Sullivan>And they'd load them up into buses or cars or whatever ICE uses and deport them. Like, that's what you hear about. So that's what he imagines about how it might go.
<v Lilly Sullivan>他们会把他们装进大巴车或汽车，或者ICE用的任何东西，然后驱逐他们。就像，这就是你听到的传闻。所以这就是他想象中可能会发生的情况。

00:25:55.480 --> 00:25:58.200
<v Roberto>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Roberto>[西班牙语交谈]

00:25:58.200 --> 00:26:07.350
<v Lilly Sullivan>On the streets, he feels a little freer, he told me, because if someone tried to grab him, he could just run. He'd have that option.
<v Lilly Sullivan>在街上，他觉得自由一点，他告诉我，因为如果有人试图抓住他，他可以跑。他会有那个选择。

00:26:07.350 --> 00:26:10.590
<v Roberto>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Roberto>[西班牙语交谈]

00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:14.620
<v Lilly Sullivan>But in the shelter, he's between four walls. There's nowhere to run.
<v Lilly Sullivan>但在收容所里，他在四堵墙之间。无处可逃。

00:26:14.620 --> 00:26:23.010
<v Roberto>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Roberto>[西班牙语交谈]

00:26:23.010 --> 00:26:35.960
<v Lilly Sullivan>I asked him, where do you feel safe? He told me, here, in the church where I talked to him. There's no other place where he can feel calm. Because he tells me, ICE can't come into the church.
<v Lilly Sullivan>我问他，你觉得在哪里安全？他告诉我，在这里，在我和他谈话的教堂里。没有别的地方能让他感到平静。因为他告诉我，ICE不能进入教堂。

00:26:35.960 --> 00:26:39.570
<v Ira Glass>Though, of course, in President Trump's next term, that might not be true.
<v Ira Glass>当然，在特朗普总统的下一个任期，那可能就不成立了。

00:26:39.570 --> 00:26:58.020
<v Lilly Sullivan>Yeah. We have this idea of sanctuary spaces. But anytime ICE decides they want to go into churches or hospitals or schools, they can do that.
<v Lilly Sullivan>是的。我们有这种避难所空间的想法。但任何时候ICE决定他们想要进入教堂、医院或学校，他们都可以那样做。

00:26:58.020 --> 00:27:21.780
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, "Nobody Loves Our Latino Community and Our Puerto Rican Community More than I Do." So Donald Trump won record numbers of Latino voters this year, more than any Republican candidate since they started tracking that demographic in exit polls back in the 1970s. So how'd it happen? Ike Sriskandarajah spent the day with a guy who's been gunning for this for years.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，“没有人比我更爱我们的拉丁裔社区和波多黎各社区”。所以唐纳德·特朗普今年赢得了创纪录数量的拉丁裔选民，比自1970年代出口民调开始跟踪该人口群体以来的任何共和党候选人都多。这是怎么发生的？艾克·斯里斯坎达拉贾花了一天时间与一个多年来一直致力于此的人在一起。

00:27:21.780 --> 00:27:43.680
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>I first met Sam Negron a few weeks ago outside a Trump rally in Allentown, Pennsylvania. He's waiting in the VIP line because, one, he's a constable. That's like an elected sheriff. And, two, he volunteers a lot for the campaign-- knocks on doors, does phone calls, is something called a Trump Force Captain, and works with Latinos for Trump.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>几周前，我在宾夕法尼亚州阿伦敦的一次特朗普集会外第一次见到山姆·内格龙。他在VIP队伍里排队，因为，第一，他是治安官。那就像民选的警长。而且，第二，他为竞选活动做了很多志愿服务——挨家挨户敲门、打电话，是所谓的“特朗普力量队长”，并与“拉丁裔支持特朗普”组织合作。

00:27:43.680 --> 00:27:44.530
<v Sam Negron>It's nice to meet you. Sam.
<v Sam Negron>很高兴见到你，山姆。

00:27:44.530 --> 00:27:45.240
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Ike. Sam?
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>艾克。山姆？

00:27:45.240 --> 00:27:45.820
<v Sam Negron>Yes, sir.
<v Sam Negron>是的，先生。

00:27:45.820 --> 00:27:46.140
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Pleasure.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>荣幸。

00:27:46.140 --> 00:27:48.590
<v Sam Negron>I never had no cool nicknames like Pookie or Tito.
<v Sam Negron>我从来没有像普基或铁托那样酷的绰号。

00:27:48.590 --> 00:27:49.500
<v Sam Negron>[LAUGHTER]
<v Sam Negron>[笑声]

00:27:49.500 --> 00:27:51.570
<v Sam Negron>It's just Sam.
<v Sam Negron>就是山姆。

00:27:51.570 --> 00:28:06.780
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Sam's a proud Puerto Rican-- big guy, classic cop haircut. And he gets personal fast. When I tell him Ike is my nickname, he says, we tend to do that in America-- shorten things. Then he calls me his brother.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>山姆是一位自豪的波多黎各人——个子大，典型的警察发型。而且他很快就变得私人化了。当我告诉他艾克是我的绰号时，他说，我们在美国倾向于那样做——缩短名字。然后他叫我兄弟。

00:28:06.780 --> 00:28:09.100
<v Sam Negron>Go ahead, brother. You should be recording this.
<v Sam Negron>来吧，兄弟。你应该录下这个。

00:28:09.100 --> 00:28:26.980
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>We're surrounded by long lines of Trump supporters who start chanting because they see a protest coming down the street. It's maybe a couple dozen people waving Puerto Rican flags. They're with a Latinx social justice group here to protest the "island of garbage" comment that a comedian recently made at a Trump rally.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>我们被长长的特朗普支持者队伍包围，他们看到街对面有抗议活动就开始高呼。那是大约几十个人挥舞着波多黎各旗帜。他们是一个拉丁裔社会正义团体，来到这里抗议一位喜剧演员最近在特朗普集会上发表的“垃圾岛”言论。

00:28:26.980 --> 00:28:27.780
<v Protestors>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Protestors>[西班牙语交谈]

00:28:27.780 --> 00:28:28.610
<v Woman>Puerto Rico!
<v Woman>波多黎各！

00:28:28.610 --> 00:28:29.820
<v Protestors>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Protestors>[西班牙语交谈]

00:28:29.820 --> 00:28:30.610
<v Woman>Puerto Rico!
<v Woman>波多黎各！

00:28:30.610 --> 00:28:33.120
<v Protestors>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Protestors>[西班牙语交谈]

00:28:33.120 --> 00:28:42.600
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>A couple of Sam's friends from Latinos for Trump rush over. They're going to cross the street to go confront the anti-Trump protesters. And they ask Sam to join.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>几个来自“拉丁裔支持特朗普”的山姆的朋友冲了过去。他们要过马路去与反特朗普抗议者对峙。他们要求山姆加入。

00:28:42.600 --> 00:28:49.990
<v Man>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Man>[西班牙语交谈]

00:28:49.990 --> 00:28:53.020
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>He tells them, I don't like to fight. Let them speak.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>他告诉他们，我不喜欢打架。让他们说吧。

00:28:53.020 --> 00:28:57.880
<v Sam Negron>See, but what I don't like-- to do the combating thing.
<v Sam Negron>看，但我所不喜欢的是——搞对抗。

00:28:57.880 --> 00:29:01.470
<v Woman>Go back to your own fucking country!
<v Woman>滚回你该死的国家去！

00:29:01.470 --> 00:29:03.150
<v Sam Negron>I didn't hear what they were saying, but--
<v Sam Negron>我没听见他们在说什么，但是——

00:29:03.150 --> 00:29:19.630
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Another Trump supporter nearby, a white lady in a red shirt, is shouting over us at this whole scrum of Spanish speakers, not really differentiating between the Trump supporters and the Trump protesters. She says, they don't even speak English. Go back to your own fucking country.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>附近另一位穿红衬衫的白人妇女，对着这群说西班牙语的人大喊大叫，并没有真正区分特朗普支持者和特朗普抗议者。她说，他们甚至不说英语。滚回你该死的国家去。

00:29:19.630 --> 00:29:21.040
<v Sam Negron>Yeah. See, that's not cool.
<v Sam Negron>是的。看，那不酷。

00:29:21.040 --> 00:29:21.880
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>What do you do with that?
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>你对此怎么处理？

00:29:21.880 --> 00:29:35.860
<v Sam Negron>Things like that, it's not cool. But you know what? I'm Puerto Rican, papi. I grew up in the South Bronx in the '70s. I've gotten racism from everybody. Because you see my color, right? I'm very light-skinned. Growing up in the South Bronx wasn't easy in the '70s.
<v Sam Negron>那样的事情，不酷。但你知道吗？我是波多黎各人，爸爸。我在70年代的布朗克斯南部长大。我从每个人那里都受到过种族歧视。因为你看到了我的肤色，对吧？我肤色很浅。在70年代的布朗克斯南部长大并不容易。

00:29:35.860 --> 00:29:39.540
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>It doesn't get under your skin, is what you're saying?
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>你的意思是，这不会让你感到困扰？

00:29:39.540 --> 00:29:54.030
<v Sam Negron>No. So I tend to ignore that. You know what I found out working on this campaign? That is not the majority.
<v Sam Negron>不。所以我倾向于忽略它。你知道我在这次竞选中发现了什么吗？那不是大多数。

00:29:54.030 --> 00:30:24.750
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>For most of his life, Sam supported Democrats. But in 2016, he broke with the party, protest voted for Jill Stein. He got into Trump from videos shared by a Latino Trump supporter. Then, just in the past two years, he lost both his son and his son-in-law. They overdosed on fentanyl. He says it's a big problem in Allentown. And he felt like the Democrats and Biden weren't helping. It made him want Trump back even more.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>山姆一生中大部分时间都支持民主党。但在2016年，他与该党分道扬镳，抗议投票给吉尔·斯坦。他通过一位拉丁裔特朗普支持者分享的视频开始接触特朗普。然后，就在过去两年里，他失去了儿子和女婿。他们因芬太尼过量而死。他说这是阿伦敦的一个大问题。他觉得民主党和拜登没有提供帮助。这让他更想让特朗普回来。

00:30:24.750 --> 00:30:48.750
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>So he started evangelizing, trying to convert people. He's got lit and signs-- Boricuas for Trump, Dominicans for Trump. He's got these little scratch-offs for Trump. It was lonely at first in this Democratic city, but slowly, it started to feel like it was working. He'd been stepping it up for months. And then "island of garbage" dropped on him.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>所以他开始传教，试图转化人们。他有传单和标语——“波多黎各人支持特朗普”、“多米尼加人支持特朗普”。他有这些特朗普的小刮刮乐。起初在这个民主党城市很孤独，但慢慢地，它开始感觉奏效了。他几个月来一直在加强攻势。然后“垃圾岛”的言论落在了他身上。

00:30:48.750 --> 00:30:56.900
<v Sam Negron>I hear my wife going, look what just came out. Look what they said. She was furious.
<v Sam Negron>我听到我妻子说，看刚刚发生了什么。看他们说了什么。她很愤怒。

00:30:56.900 --> 00:31:07.830
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>It was hurtful because that's where they're from, but also frustrating, because he's been out here in these streets, trying to pull his people one by one towards Trump.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>这很伤人，因为那是他们来自的地方，但也令人沮丧，因为他一直在这里，在大街上，试图把他的同胞一个接一个地拉向特朗普。

00:31:07.830 --> 00:31:17.340
<v Sam Negron>I was double upset because I'm like, one, that's an ugly joke. Two, there goes all the work I've done for the last four or five years.
<v Sam Negron>我加倍沮丧，因为我像，第一，那是个丑陋的笑话。第二，我过去四五年所做的所有工作都白费了。

00:31:17.340 --> 00:31:21.540
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Did you think those comments might just cost him the whole election?
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>你认为那些言论可能会让他输掉整个选举吗？

00:31:21.540 --> 00:31:27.280
<v Sam Negron>Totally, brother. I said, oh, my god, there goes everything we've done-- [LAUGHS] right out the window.
<v Sam Negron>完全是，兄弟。我说，天哪，我们所做的一切都随着——[笑] 窗外去了。

00:31:27.280 --> 00:31:36.560
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>So how to reconcile all this, being a proud Puerto Rican and a die-hard Trump supporter? For Sam, it took 20 minutes of googling.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>所以如何调和所有这一切，作为一个自豪的波多黎各人和坚定的特朗普支持者？对山姆来说，这花了20分钟的谷歌搜索。

00:31:36.560 --> 00:31:42.220
<v Sam Negron>Anytime something controversial comes out of that campaign, there's something else behind it. You know what I found out?
<v Sam Negron>每当那个竞选活动传出有争议的事情时，背后总有别的东西。你知道我发现了什么吗？

00:31:42.220 --> 00:31:47.270
