00:40:03.920 --> 00:40:08.220
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Sam did talk to one Harris supporter, a younger Dominican man.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>山姆确实和一个哈里斯支持者谈过，一个年轻的多米尼加人。

00:40:08.220 --> 00:40:14.270
<v Sam Negron>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Sam Negron>[西班牙语交谈]

00:40:14.270 --> 00:40:25.100
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>The man says he can't vote. He'd like to become a citizen, vote someday. Again, this is where a traditional canvasser would pivot. That's not Sam.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>这个人说他不能投票。他想成为公民，有一天投票。再次，这是传统拉票者会转向的地方。那不是山姆。

00:40:25.100 --> 00:40:32.850
<v Sam Negron>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Sam Negron>[西班牙语交谈]

00:40:32.850 --> 00:40:47.120
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>If he could vote, he'd vote for Kamala. Hey, there's one. Now, this close to the election, campaigns are a numbers game of maximizing turnout of eligible, like-minded voters. This guy is neither.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>如果他能投票，他会投票给卡马拉。嘿，有一个。现在，离选举这么近，竞选活动是最大化合格、志同道合选民投票率的数字游戏。这个人两者都不是。

00:40:47.120 --> 00:40:54.620
<v Sam Negron>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Sam Negron>[西班牙语交谈]

00:40:54.620 --> 00:40:59.970
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>The guy says he wants a change. Sam sees an opening-- a change from what?
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>这个人说他想要改变。山姆看到了一个机会——改变什么？

00:40:59.970 --> 00:41:15.950
<v Man>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Man>[西班牙语交谈]

00:41:15.950 --> 00:41:19.700
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Sam makes his pitch for Trump and asks him to think about it.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>山姆为特朗普做了他的推销，并请他考虑一下。

00:41:19.700 --> 00:41:27.240
<v Man>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Man>[西班牙语交谈]

00:41:27.240 --> 00:41:41.630
<v Sam Negron>[LAUGHS] I left him thinking. He's almost flipped. My whole mission is to wake people up in my community. To me, it's not about the vote. To me, it's about our people. And that's the way I look at it.
<v Sam Negron>[笑] 我留他在思考。他几乎被翻转了。我的整个使命是唤醒我们社区的人们。对我来说，这与投票无关。对我来说，这与我们的人民有关。而这就是我看它的方式。

00:41:41.630 --> 00:41:59.150
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>To Sam, it's the long game. This isn't just about the election that just happened. It's about the next one and the next one and the one after that.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>对山姆来说，这是长期的博弈。这不仅仅是关于刚刚发生的选举。这是关于下一次和下一次以及下一次之后的那一次。

00:41:59.150 --> 00:42:02.870
<v Ira Glass>Ike Sriskandarajah is a producer on our show.
<v Ira Glass>艾克·斯里斯坎达拉贾是我们节目的制作人。

00:42:02.870 --> 00:42:18.890
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, all those people on Trump's enemies list who he's been calling for retribution against, are they packing their bags? We check in with a couple. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>即将到来，特朗普名单上的那些他一直在呼吁报复的敌人，他们正在打包行李吗？我们与几个人核实。那是一分钟后，当我们的节目继续时，来自芝加哥公共广播电台。

00:42:18.890 --> 00:42:33.870
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program-- "This is the Cake We Baked." Today, we have stories of people who have some special, personal investment in the outcome of this week's election, and we look at what's coming for them. We have arrived at Act Three of our show.
<v Ira Glass>它是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目——“这是我们烤的蛋糕”。今天，我们有对本周选举结果有特殊、个人投资的人的故事，我们看看对他们来说会发生什么。我们已经到达了我们节目的第三幕。

00:42:33.870 --> 00:43:04.010
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, "Sometimes Revenge Can Be Justified." So in the final weeks of his campaign, Donald Trump only stepped up his threats of retribution against his political enemies, including saying he'd use the military to go after the enemy within. Two people who've been keeping an eye on these sorts of comments are Alexander and Rachel Vindman. Alex is the army officer who officially reported what seemed like an improper phone call between President Trump and the president of Ukraine, which led to Trump's first impeachment trial. Rachel is his wife.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，“有时候报复是可以证明的”。所以在竞选的最后几周，唐纳德·特朗普只是加剧了他对政治敌人的报复威胁，包括说他会使用军队去对付内部的敌人。一直关注这类评论的两个人是亚历山大和瑞秋·温德曼。亚历克斯是正式报告看起来像唐纳德·特朗普总统和乌克兰总统之间不当通话的陆军军官，这导致了特朗普的第一次弹劾审判。瑞秋是他的妻子。

00:43:04.010 --> 00:43:17.490
<v Ira Glass>Back in March, I visited them. And they did not agree about what they were going to do if President Trump won another term and started taking revenge. Rachel said they should consider leaving the country if things got bad enough. Alex said he'd never consider that.
<v Ira Glass>三月回国，我拜访了他们。他们不同意如果唐纳德·特朗普赢得了另一个任期并开始报复他们会做什么。瑞秋说如果情况变得足够糟糕，他们应该考虑离开这个国家。亚历克斯说他永远不会考虑这一点。

00:43:17.490 --> 00:43:26.390
<v Alexander Vindman>No, not possible. I think the fact is that I served a full military career to protect this country, and I'm not going to give it up.
<v Alexander Vindman>不，不可能。我认为事实是我服役了完整的军事生涯以保护这个国家，我不会放弃它。

00:43:26.390 --> 00:43:42.000
<v Rachel Vindman>My argument is, if Donald Trump is reelected, this will no longer be that country. This will be the place where that country was. I want to be able to leave if I feel like that's what I need to, to protect mainly my daughter.
<v Rachel Vindman>我的论点是，如果唐纳德·特朗普再次当选，这将不再是那个国家。这将是那个国家所在的地方。如果我觉得那是保护我女儿所需，我想要能够离开。

00:43:42.000 --> 00:43:50.750
<v Ira Glass>We were doing this interview in their living room. And we were standing, because they hadn't bought furniture for it yet. They wanted to wait and see what the outcome of the election was going to be first.
<v Ira Glass>我们在他们的客厅里进行这次采访。而且我们站着，因为他们还没给它买家具。他们想等到选举结果出来先。

00:43:50.750 --> 00:44:13.470
<v Ira Glass>For Alex, this was just prudence and saving money against an uncertain future. For Rachel, it was, maybe we'll have to leave this house. When I reached out this week after the election, Alex didn't return my text to set up an interview, but Rachel was happy to talk. She told me they did finally bite the bullet and buy furniture in those early days when Kamala Harris took over as the Democratic nominee.
<v Ira Glass>对亚历克斯来说，这只是谨慎和针对不确定未来的省钱。对瑞秋来说，是，也许我们将不得不离开这所房子。当我在选举后本周联系时，亚历克斯没有回复我的短信来安排采访，但瑞秋很高兴谈谈。她告诉我他们确实终于咬牙在卡马拉·哈里斯接手成为民主党提名人的那些早些日子买了家具。

00:44:13.470 --> 00:44:32.190
<v Rachel Vindman>We have two sofas, and we have a coffee table and a side piece thing. I don't know. But that's [INAUDIBLE]. We ordered it after all the buzz about her, and everyone was very excited. And we were just like, we're going to stay here. And I think we were very optimistic. So--
<v Rachel Vindman>我们有两个沙发，我们有一个咖啡桌和一个侧面的东西。我不知道。但那是[听不清]。我们在关于她的所有炒作后订购了它，每个人都很兴奋。而且我们只是像，我们要留在这里。而且我认为我们非常乐观。所以——

00:44:32.190 --> 00:44:45.690
<v Ira Glass>In the months leading up to the election, Alex and Rachel have both been campaigning actively for Harris, speaking at fundraisers, that kind of thing. Rachel says she's probably done three events a week for the last six weeks, on video or in person, in all seven swing states.
<v Ira Glass>在选举前的几个月里，亚历克斯和瑞秋都一直在积极为哈里斯竞选，在筹款活动中发言，那种事情。瑞秋说她过去六周大概每周做了三个活动，在视频或现场，在所有七个摇摆州。

00:44:45.690 --> 00:45:03.630
<v Ira Glass>We'd had plans to talk on election night once the winner was clear. But when I texted them, Rachel wrote back she didn't feel up to talking. "I am freaking out," she said. When we got on the phone later in the week, I asked if their views had changed since we talked in March. Was it still that she would consider leaving the country and he wouldn't?
<v Ira Glass>我们本来计划在选举之夜一旦获胜者清楚就交谈。但当我给他们发短信时，瑞秋回信说她没心情谈。“我在崩溃，”她说。当我们稍后在周通过电话时，我问他们的观点是否从我们在三月交谈以来改变了。还是她会考虑离开这个国家而他不？

00:45:03.630 --> 00:45:37.180
<v Rachel Vindman>Nothing is off the table, but I want to stay, because this is my country. And I think there's a possibility to continue to be here and to be part of the discussion and make a difference. Alex absolutely wants to stay. It's not even a question. So maybe my decision is just based on his obstinance that it's a non-starter. So I have to bring myself around to that reality.
<v Rachel Vindman>没有什么是不可能的，但我想要留下来，因为这是我的国家。而且我认为有一个可能性继续留在这里并成为讨论的一部分并产生影响。亚历克斯绝对想要留下来。这甚至不是一个问题。所以也许我的决定只是基于他的固执，这是一个非启动器。所以我必须把自己带到那个现实。

00:45:37.180 --> 00:45:59.120
<v Ira Glass>I thought of you guys in the last weeks of the campaign, when it seemed like Donald Trump was increasing his calls for retaliation and talking about going after the enemies within the country. What do the two of you think this is going to mean for you? Do you feel like you're going to be facing some sort of retaliation? Do you feel like it's just a complete open question? Where are you on this?
<v Ira Glass>在竞选的最后几周我想到你们，当看起来唐纳德·特朗普正在增加他对报复的呼吁并谈论去追究国家内部的敌人。你们两个认为这对你们意味着什么？你们觉得你们将面临某种报复吗？你们觉得这只是一个完全开放的问题吗？你们在这里处于什么位置？

00:45:59.120 --> 00:46:20.040
<v Rachel Vindman>We have no idea. It will depend on signals from their actions. That's the only way we'll know, is to see what they're doing. But there is this guy who has a retaliation list. It's a long list. It's, like, 300, 350 people, something like that. And Alex's name is on it.
<v Rachel Vindman>我们不知道。这将取决于他们行动的信号。那是我们知道的唯一方式，是看他们在做什么。但有这个家伙有一个报复名单。这是一个长名单。它，像，300，350人，那样的东西。而且亚历克斯的名字在上面。

00:46:20.040 --> 00:46:40.890
<v Ira Glass>This is a list that was circulating on social media, put together by a Trump supporter, the people that he thinks deserve revenge. He has no affiliation with Trump. There's no sign that anybody in Trump world listens to him. But seeing Alex's name there was alarming, a reminder of how visible Alex is as somebody who stood up to the president and how likely a target he is for the real people in power.
<v Ira Glass>这是一个在社交媒体上流传的名单，由一个特朗普支持者汇总，他认为值得报复的人。他与特朗普没有任何关系。没有任何迹象表明特朗普世界里的任何人听他的。但看到亚历克斯的名字在那里是令人担忧的，一个关于亚历克斯作为站起来反对总统的人是多么可见的提醒，以及他作为有权力的真人的目标有多么可能。

00:46:40.890 --> 00:47:01.350
<v Rachel Vindman>So that's a concern. But are they going to retaliate against all of those people? Will it be all at once, like Kristallnacht, or will it be over time? And do we have time if that's the road they go down? I don't know. Or will they just do it on January 20?
<v Rachel Vindman>所以那是一个担忧。但他们会对所有那些人报复吗？这将是一次全部，像水晶之夜，还是会随时间？而且如果那是他们走下的路，我们有时间吗？我不知道。还是他们会在1月20日做它？

00:47:01.350 --> 00:47:02.310
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:47:02.310 --> 00:47:22.740
<v Rachel Vindman>But we'll definitely be watching the indicators-- and a list of criteria to say if this, then this. Some kind of places or inflection points, which is terrifying. But we certainly haven't gotten to that point. Like everyone, we're trying to process all of this.
<v Rachel Vindman>但我们将肯定会关注指标——以及如果这个，那么这个的标准列表。某种地方或转折点，这是可怕的。但我们当然还没到那一点。像每个人一样，我们正在试图消化所有这些。

00:47:22.740 --> 00:47:26.160
<v Ira Glass>Can you name another one of the indicators that you're going to keep an eye on?
<v Ira Glass>你能说出你们要关注的另一个指标吗？

00:47:26.160 --> 00:47:43.170
<v Rachel Vindman>Let's say ramp up talks about retaliation, then yes. But maybe it was election rhetoric and that's not something that's going to come up. There are so many unknowns with Trump. He says he's going to do a lot of things that he doesn't do.
<v Rachel Vindman>让我们说加紧谈论报复，那么是的。但也许那是竞选修辞，那不是将会出现的东西。特朗普有那么多未知数。他说他要做很多他没做的事。

00:47:43.170 --> 00:48:03.180
<v Ira Glass>They're going to keep an eye on his appointments, especially who he appoints as Secretary of Defense. Rachel pictures that retaliation against Alex could be as small as they try to take his retirement benefits, his health care, pension, or as big as they could put him on trial for his actions in the impeachment hearings. She really doesn't know what to expect.
<v Ira Glass>他们将关注他的任命，特别是他任命谁作为国防部长。瑞秋设想针对亚历克斯的报复可能小到他们试图拿走他的退休福利、他的医疗保健、养老金，或大到他们可以因为他在弹劾听证会中的行动而对他进行审判。她真的不知道该期待什么。

00:48:03.180 --> 00:48:08.130
<v Ira Glass>What's your feeling? What's your gut?
<v Ira Glass>你的感觉是什么？你的直觉是什么？

00:48:08.130 --> 00:48:40.980
<v Rachel Vindman>My head says it'll probably be OK. My heart is very scared. But I don't want to live my life in fear. I don't want to live in my own country afraid when neither I nor my husband has done anything wrong.
<v Rachel Vindman>我的头脑说它可能没问题。我的心非常害怕。但我不想在恐惧中生活。我不想在自己的国家感到害怕，当我既没有也没有我的丈夫做任何错事时。

00:48:40.980 --> 00:48:42.450
<v Ira Glass>Rachel Vindman.
<v Ira Glass>瑞秋·温德曼。

00:48:42.450 --> 00:49:14.550
<v Ira Glass>We reached out to the other people we interviewed in March for the episode we did about possible retribution Donald Trump might take to see how concerned they are about what might happen to them now. Former Trump aide Stephanie Grisham didn't return our calls. Fred Wellman, who used to be at the Lincoln Project, said he was watching to see how things were going to unfold. And he's gaming out possible scenarios for what he might do.
<v Ira Glass>我们联系了我们在三月采访的其他人为我们制作的关于唐纳德·特朗普可能采取的可能报复的情节，看看他们有多关注现在可能发生在他们身上的事情。前特朗普助手斯蒂芬妮·格里沙姆没有回复我们的电话。曾经在林肯项目的弗雷德·韦尔曼说他正在观看事情将如何展开。而且他正在针对他可能做的事情制定可能的场景。

00:49:14.550 --> 00:49:41.190
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, "I've Done an Unbelievable Job on the Abortion Question." So I think, as everybody knows at this point, Kamala Harris and the Democrats were betting that abortion rights would help put her over the top. And 10 different states had abortion rights measures on their ballots. Seven of those passed. And there was a very particular kind of TV ad that aired in many of those states, all with a very particular feeling and particular music, very similar language.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，“我在堕胎问题上做了一项难以置信的工作”。所以我认为，正如每个人在这一点上都知道的，卡马拉·哈里斯和民主党人是在打赌堕胎权会帮助她越过顶端。而10个不同的州在他们的选票上有堕胎权措施。七个通过了。而且有一种非常特定的电视广告在许多那些州播出，都有一个非常特定的感觉和特定的音乐，非常相似的语言。

00:49:41.190 --> 00:49:59.310
<v Ira Glass>In these ads, women whose lives have been put at risk from complications with their pregnancies told their stories to explain why abortion bans needed to be lifted. One of our producers, Miki Meek, became interested in these commercials. And this week, she checked in with a few of the people who appeared in them. Here's Miki.
<v Ira Glass>在这些广告中，生活因怀孕并发症而处于危险中的妇女讲述她们的故事来解释为什么堕胎禁令需要被解除。我们的制作人之一，米基·米克，对这些商业广告产生了兴趣。而本周，她核实了几个出现在其中的人。这是米基。

00:49:59.310 --> 00:50:18.160
<v Miki Meek>Since Roe was struck down two years ago, I've been reporting on pregnant women and doctors in states with abortion bans. And I've noticed that it's almost always the same group of women, about 20 in all, being called on again and again to recount the worst moments of their lives in media interviews, in court cases, at their state legislatures, and in Congress.
<v Miki Meek>自从罗诉韦德案两年前被推翻以来，我一直在报道各州的孕妇和医生有堕胎禁令。而且我注意到几乎总是同一组妇女，约20人，被一次又一次地叫出来在媒体采访、法庭案件、在他们的州立法机构和国会中叙述她们生活中最糟糕的时刻。

00:50:18.160 --> 00:50:21.180
<v Kimberly Paseka>I don't love it, to be perfectly honest. I feel like it's really--
<v Kimberly Paseka>我不喜欢它，要完全诚实。我觉得它真的很——

00:50:21.180 --> 00:50:27.070
<v Miki Meek>That's Kimberly Paseka, who was denied an abortion in Nebraska. She shared her story in one of those ads.
<v Miki Meek>那是金伯利·帕塞卡，她在内布拉斯加州被拒绝堕胎。她在那些广告之一中分享了她的故事。

00:50:27.070 --> 00:51:02.880
<v Kimberly Paseka>It's disheartening that we are putting the toughest conversation on the people who are affected the most-- and having to relive that trauma and just relive the worst parts of their life to try and make change. And even today, I'm sitting here, and I'm exhausted. It's like, how am I even standing here still? The last two-plus months has just been exhausting. And you see online and people are like, we have to keep fighting. And I'm like, I have been fighting. Where have you been?
<v Kimberly Paseka>令人沮丧的是，我们把最艰难的对话放在最受影响的人身上——并且必须重温那种创伤，只是重温她们生活中最糟糕的部分来试图做出改变。即使今天，我坐在这里，而且我很疲惫。它像，我甚至还站在那里？过去两加月只是令人疲惫。而且你在网上看到，人们像，我们必须继续战斗。而且我像，我一直在战斗。你在哪里？

00:51:02.880 --> 00:51:05.870
<v Miki Meek>Nebraska's abortion rights measure failed this week.
<v Miki Meek>内布拉斯加州的堕胎权措施本周失败了。

00:51:05.870 --> 00:51:14.710
<v Kimberly Paseka>I actually-- I made the joke to my husband yesterday. I was like, all my new followers on Twitter are going to be so disappointed when I start just posting about basketball and football again.
<v Kimberly Paseka>我实际上——我昨天给我的丈夫开了个玩笑。我像，我在推特上的所有新粉丝都会如此失望，当我开始再次只是发布关于篮球和足球时。

00:51:14.710 --> 00:51:17.140
<v Kimberly Paseka>[LAUGHTER]
<v Kimberly Paseka>[笑声]

00:51:17.140 --> 00:51:45.790
<v Kimberly Paseka>Until we resolve the issue, I feel like I'll always say something. But I am going to have to step away from it for a little bit, just for my sanity, I think. I don't think I'll ever stop pushing how important the issue is, though, because until there aren't any bans, some woman's always going to be affected. Some girl's going to be affected. And I hate that for this country.
<v Kimberly Paseka>直到我们解决这个问题，我觉得我总会说些什么。但我将不得不从它稍微退后一点，只是为了我的理智，我想。我不认为我会停止推动问题的重要性，虽然，因为直到没有任何禁令，一些妇女总是会受到影响。一些女孩会受到影响。而且我恨这个国家。

00:51:45.790 --> 00:52:13.510
<v Kimberly Paseka>Of all the ads I watched, Deborah Dorbert's really got to me. It was so emotional I almost couldn't finish it. Debra's 34 years old and from Florida. She's got a six-year-old son. Two years ago, she was 23 weeks pregnant with her second child when she went into her doctor's office for a routine scan and learned that her baby had a lethal and rare condition called Potter Syndrome. He had no kidneys. His lungs weren't developing. If he survived birth, he wouldn't live long, maybe just minutes.
<v Kimberly Paseka>在我看过的所有广告中，黛博拉·多伯特的真正得到了我。它太情绪化了，我几乎不能完成它。黛博拉34岁，来自佛罗里达。她有一个六岁的儿子。两年前，她怀着第二个孩子23周时，她去她的医生办公室进行常规扫描并了解到她的婴儿有一种致命且罕见的病症叫波特综合征。他没有肾脏。他的肺部没有发育。如果他出生存活，他不会活很长时间，也许只是几分钟。

00:52:13.510 --> 00:52:36.070
<v Kimberly Paseka>Her doctor recommended she terminate the pregnancy as soon as possible. She was now at higher risk for preeclampsia, which could kill her. Deborah agreed. She also didn't want her baby to suffer. But Florida had just banned all abortions after 15 weeks, with some rare and vague exceptions. Deborah says the hospital told her that because her baby still had a heartbeat, they couldn't help her.
<v Kimberly Paseka>她的医生建议她尽快终止妊娠。她现在处于先兆子痫的高风险中，这可能会杀死她。黛博拉同意了。她也不想她的婴儿受苦。但佛罗里达州刚刚禁止了15周后的所有堕胎，有一些罕见和模糊的例外。黛博拉说医院告诉她，因为她的婴儿仍然有心跳，他们不能帮她。

00:52:36.070 --> 00:52:59.770
<v Kimberly Paseka>They were actually wrong about this. Florida's law allows for abortions in cases where there's a lethal fetal anomaly up to 28 weeks. But there's a lot of fear and confusion among doctors and hospitals in states with bans. So Deborah had to carry the baby to term, knowing all along he wouldn't survive. She and her husband named him Milo. He lived for 94 minutes.
<v Kimberly Paseka>他们实际上对此错了。佛罗里达州的法律允许在有致命胎儿异常的情况下进行堕胎，直到28周。但有大量的恐惧和混乱在有禁令的州的医生和医院之间。所以黛博拉不得不把孩子带到足月，一直知道他不会存活。她和她的丈夫给他取名叫米洛。他活了94分钟。

00:52:59.770 --> 00:53:21.310
<v Kimberly Paseka>Deborah's husband told me he didn't expect her to speak publicly, seemed out of character. And it felt that way to her too. Deborah describes herself as shy, reserved, conflict-averse. But while she was still pregnant with Milo, she was so angry and depressed that she decided to talk to a reporter from the Washington Post. And after she gave birth, she decided to keep talking.
<v Kimberly Paseka>黛博拉的丈夫告诉我，他没期望她公开说话，对她来说似乎不符合性格。而且那对她来说也是那种感觉。黛博拉形容自己害羞、矜持、回避冲突。但在她仍然怀着米洛时，她太生气和沮丧，以至于她决定和一个来自华盛顿邮报的记者谈谈。而且在她生下孩子后，她决定继续谈话。

00:53:21.310 --> 00:53:29.740
<v Miki Meek>Is there a specific conversation or interaction you had with someone where you realized, I did change their opinion about Florida's abortion law?
<v Miki Meek>有没有一个特定的对话或互动你和一个意识到的人，我确实改变了他们关于佛罗里达堕胎法的意见？

00:53:29.740 --> 00:54:07.150
<v Deborah Dorbert>My parents. My parents, they are very conservative people. And it was my dad, having witnessed what I went through when-- he found out the news, and then he found out that I could not get induced and I was being forced to carry to full term. And he had to watch his daughter suffer for months, and he didn't understand why I could not get the care I needed.
<v Deborah Dorbert>我的父母。我的父母，他们是非常保守的人。而且那是我的父亲，目睹了我就算经历了什么——他发现了新闻，然后他发现我不能被引产，而且我被迫怀到足月。而且他不得不看着他的女儿受苦数月，而且他不理解为什么我不能得到我需要的照顾。

00:54:07.150 --> 00:54:22.570
<v Miki Meek>Her parents are Catholic, and before this, they were against abortion. But seeing her experience up close changed them. So when the organizers of the ballot measure in Florida approached her to do a TV ad, she said yes. The measure would put abortion rights into the state constitution.
<v Miki Meek>她的父母是天主教徒，而且在此之前，他们反对堕胎。但近距离看到她的经历改变了他们。所以当佛罗里达州投票措施的组织者接近她做电视广告时，她说好。该措施将把堕胎权放入州宪法。

00:54:22.570 --> 00:54:36.100
<v Deborah Dorbert>I remember the doctor handing me a baby boy that was blue, and I just held him because he was so cold.
<v Deborah Dorbert>我记得医生递给我一个蓝色的男婴，我只是抱着他，因为他太冷了。

00:54:36.100 --> 00:54:43.340
<v Miki Meek>In the ad, Deborah is sitting on a couch in a green dress. Her husband, Lee, is right next to her, his arm wrapped around her.
<v Miki Meek>在广告中，黛博拉坐在绿色连衣裙的沙发上。她的丈夫，李，就在她旁边，他的手臂裹着她。

00:54:43.340 --> 00:54:55.480
<v Deborah Dorbert>Government had no right to do that to my family. This ban is torture.
<v Deborah Dorbert>政府没权利对我的家庭那样做。这个禁令是酷刑。

00:54:55.480 --> 00:55:17.560
<v Miki Meek>Governor DeSantis fought the abortion measure hard. He sent plainclothes police to knock on doors looking for fraudulent signatures on the petition for the ballot measure. The Florida Health Department sent cease and desist letters to TV stations, airing a different ad that supported the measure. A judge siding with the TV stations wrote, "To keep it simple for the state of Florida, it's the First Amendment, stupid."
<v Miki Meek>德桑蒂斯州长强硬地打击了堕胎措施。他派遣便衣警察去敲门寻找请愿书上欺诈性的签名。佛罗里达卫生部向电视站发送了停止和中止信，播出了一个支持该措施的不同广告。一个站在电视站一边的法官写道，“为了让它对佛罗里达州保持简单，这是第一修正案，愚蠢。”

00:55:17.560 --> 00:55:40.600
<v Miki Meek>On election night. Deborah and her husband headed to a watch party with organizers of the ballot measure, a group called Yes on 4. There were balloons everywhere, a full bar. Deborah felt hopeful and had a speech prepared if the measure got passed. Ever since Roe was overturned, whenever voters got a chance to vote on abortion measures like this one, they passed, even in red states.
<v Miki Meek>在选举之夜。黛博拉和她的丈夫前往一个观看派对，派对由投票措施的组织者举行，一个叫“4号是”的团体。到处都是气球，一个完整的酒吧。黛博拉感觉充满希望，如果措施获得通过，她准备了一个演讲。自从罗被推翻以来，每当选民有机会对像这样的堕胎措施进行投票时，它们就通过了，即使在红色州。

00:55:40.600 --> 00:55:55.780
<v Miki Meek>Around 9:00 PM, the results were in. A clear majority of Floridians voted in favor of the ballot initiative, but it still failed because in Florida, ballot measures need 60% of the vote to pass. It got 57%.
<v Miki Meek>晚上9:00左右，结果出来了。清楚多数佛罗里达人投票赞成投票倡议，但它仍然失败了，因为在佛罗里达，投票措施需要60%的票数才能通过。它获得了57%。

00:55:55.780 --> 00:56:24.520
<v Deborah Dorbert>We were upstairs in a private little room, just waiting for the results. And once they told me that Yes on 4 failed, it was just-- honestly, it was a shock. It was like a punch in the stomach, and it just brought me back to that day in the doctor's office. And it was just that shock and that numb.
<v Deborah Dorbert>我们在楼上一个小房间里，只是等待结果。而且一旦他们告诉我4号是失败了，它只是——老实说，这是一个震惊。它像胃部的重击，而且它只是把我带回了医生办公室的那一天。而且那只是那个震惊和那个麻木。

00:56:24.520 --> 00:56:58.690
<v Deborah Dorbert>We did go downstairs to hear the director speak. And after she spoke and a few other guests spoke, I remember getting up out of the room and going outside. And that is when I just broke down crying. And my husband came and found me. And I was on my knees and crying because it just-- the law took everything from me.
<v Deborah Dorbert>我们确实下楼去听导演说话。而且在她说话以及其他几个客人说话后，我记得我走出房间，走到外面。而且那时我只是崩溃大哭。而且我的丈夫来找到了我。而且我跪在地上哭，因为它只是——法律从我这里拿走了所有东西。

00:56:58.690 --> 00:57:06.280
<v Miki Meek>It's one thing to decide to tell this painful story over and over, but it's another thing to do it and not get the results you wanted.
<v Miki Meek>决定一次又一次地讲述这个痛苦的故事是一回事，但做它却得不到你想要的结果是另一回事。

00:57:06.280 --> 00:57:30.050
<v Deborah Dorbert>It's hard. You feel like a lot of weight is on your shoulders to try to get the government and legislation to understand, like, look what these laws are doing. I do feel tired. Just sharing my story is a lot of work.
<v Deborah Dorbert>它很难。你觉得很多重量都在你的肩上，试图让政府和立法机构理解，像，看看这些法律在做什么。我确实感到疲惫。分享我的故事是很多工作。

00:57:30.050 --> 00:57:35.230
<v Miki Meek>Do you ever have the feeling of, when can I be done and stop talking about this?
<v Miki Meek>你是否有过，什么时候我可以完成并停止谈论这个的感觉？

00:57:35.230 --> 00:58:02.420
<v Deborah Dorbert>So yesterday, we spent a lot of time navigating, do we keep fighting this fight or do we just step away? And for me, I'm up on the fence. I don't want to back down now. I want to see through that the law gets changed.
<v Deborah Dorbert>所以昨天，我们花了很多时间导航，我们继续战斗这场战斗吗还是我们只是退后？而且对我来说，我在栅栏上。我现在不想退缩。我想看到法律被改变。

00:58:02.420 --> 00:58:25.310
<v Miki Meek>What people don't see when Deborah tells her story in public is how hard she's still trying to get back to real life, just enjoy small moments again-- karate classes on Thursdays, pizza night on Fridays with her husband and her six-year-old son.
<v Miki Meek>当黛博拉公开讲述她的故事时，人们看不到的是她仍然多么努力地试图回到现实生活，只是再次享受小小的时刻——星期四的空手道课，星期五的披萨之夜与她的丈夫和她六岁的儿子。

00:58:25.310 --> 00:58:27.750
<v Ira Glass>Miki Meek is a producer on our show.
<v Ira Glass>米基·米克是我们节目的制作人。

